From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 00:57:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 075/105] hc layer boundary: defer the MoE combine into the
 next layer's mix K0

R7 folded the apply of the attention boundary's combine into the MoE
boundary's K0, because the two are consecutive statements of one method.
The other half of every layer -- the MoE combine feeding the *next*
layer's attention mix -- is the same shape of work, but the combine has
to survive a trip through the model loop to reach the K0 that would
absorb it. `_PendingHCCombine` is that trip: `_postprocess_qwen4_exp_layer`
returns the combine's inputs instead of its result, and the next layer's
`_prepare_qwen4_exp_attn` hands them to `combine_then_mix`, which runs the
apply as K0's prologue exactly as R7 does.

Two things ride along that R7 never had to carry. R6's deferred
shared-expert join is now part of the apply being moved, so the prologue
grew a `FUSE_SHARED` stage that forms `bf16(routed + gate * shared)`
before the residual update -- the same intermediate rounding
`hc_combine_apply`'s `kUseShared` path does, so the two agree bitwise.
And the PLE, whose query *reads* the combined residual, cannot have the
apply moved past it; layer 1 (the only PLE layer here) and the last layer
keep the plain apply, which leaves 46 of 48 boundaries fused per target
forward. The draft backbone is one layer, so it contributes none.

Numerics on real weights, M in {1, 4, 16}, bf16 and fp8 mix weights, with
and without the shared fold: `applied`, `normed` and the gate partials are
bit-identical to apply-then-mix in all 12 cases. `mixed` differs on a few
elements in 2 of 12, but K1 accumulates `t_raw` with device-scope atomics
and is already run-to-run non-deterministic on identical inputs (measured:
5/5 repeats differ at M=16 bf16), so that is the atomics, not the fold.

Default off (`SGLANG_HC_LAYER_APPLY_FUSED`).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
